  ],
  "text": "Every crop you eat is a mutant. We picked them out on purpose. A wild grass wants to scatter its seed. That is the entire point of it. So the moment the seed is ripe, the stalk shatters and drops it. Which is useless to anyone harvesting. You arrive and the ground already has it. But occasionally one plant carries a mutation and does not shatter. It just holds on. In the wild that plant is a failure. It is also the only one you can carry home. So you harvest it, you plant its seed, and within centuries it is all there is. Corn came from a grass called teosinte, with about a dozen hard little kernels. Broccoli, cabbage, kale, sprouts and cauliflower are one wild plant, pushed six directions. This is genetic engineering. It just had a ten thousand year head start. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
